    ('manufacturing_worker_rights_freedom_association', 'Manufacturing Worker Rights - Freedom of Association and Wage Theft', 'ILO C087 (Freedom of Association, 1948) Art. 2; ILO C098 (Right to Organise, 1949); ILO C029 Indicator 6 (intimidation and threats); ILO C095 Art. 8; ILO C131 (Minimum Wage Fixing, 1970); EU Corporate Sustainability Due Diligence Directive (CSDDD) 2024; US UFLPA 2021',
     "Manufacturing (garments, electronics, food processing) concentrates migrant workers in export processing zones where national labour law is sometimes suspended or under-enforced. Core entitlements: (1) Workers have the right to form or join trade unions and to bargain collectively regardless of their migration status - ILO C087 Art. 2 is unconditional; EPZ carve-outs that exclude union rights violate C087 and C098. (2) Wages must be paid at least at the applicable statutory minimum - piece-rate or line-speed systems must deliver the floor wage per hour actually worked (ILO C131). (3) Itemised wage statements for every pay period - workers must be able to verify their hours and deductions. (4) Workers cannot be compelled to pay for dormitory accommodation or canteen meals that reduce their net take-home below the minimum wage (ILO C095 Art. 4 limits in-kind wages). (5) Managers cannot threaten loss of employment, immigration status, or repatriation to suppress complaints - this is ILO Indicator 6 (intimidation and threats) and meets the Palermo Protocol 'threat of penalty' threshold. Red flags: workers required to sign attendance sheets that under-record actual hours; productivity bonuses structured so they are never achievable and serve as a pretext to pay below minimum; dormitory access cards withheld as a disciplinary measure; workers told they will be 'blacklisted' from the industry if they complain. The EU CSDDD (2024) imposes due-diligence obligations on EU companies sourcing from manufacturing supply chains, creating an accountability lever for advocates in destination-state courts."),
